    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b><h3>Worked Solution</h3></b></p>
<p>1. To evaluate \(f(0)\), we substitute \(x = 0\) into the function \(f(x)\): \(f(0) = \frac{\cos(0) - 1}{0} = \frac{1 - 1}{0}\)</p>
<p>However, this expression is undefined as it involves division by zero. Therefore, \(f(0)\) does not have a finite value.</p>
<p>2. Now, let's fill in the table to find \(\displaystyle \lim_{{x \to 0}} f(x)\):</p>
<table>
<caption></caption>
<thead>
<tr>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">\(x\)</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center; border: 1px solid black;">\(-0.5\)</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center; border: 1px solid black;">\(0.5\)</th><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">\(-0.25\)<br></th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center; border: 1px solid black;">\(0.25\)<br></th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center; border: 1px solid black;">\(-0.1\)<br></th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center; border: 1px solid black;">\(0.1\)<br></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">\(f(x)\)</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispsf(ta2,3)@}<br></td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispsf(ta3,3)@}<br></td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispsf(ta4,3)@}<br></td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispsf(ta5,3)@}<br></td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispsf(ta6,3)@}<br></td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispsf(ta7,3)@}<br></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<br>
<p>3. The value of \(\displaystyle \lim_{{x \to 0}} f(x)\) represents the limit of the function \(f(x)\) as \(x\) approaches \(0\). </p>
<p>To find this limit, we can use either direct substitution (which may not work in this case due to division by zero) or the L'Hôpital's Rule.</p>
<p>L'Hôpital's Rule states that if the limit of a function \( \displaystyle \frac{g(x)}{h(x)}\) as \(x\) approaches a certain value is of the form \(\frac{0}{0}\) or \( \displaystyle \frac{\infty}{\infty}\), then the limit of the function can be found by taking the derivative of both \(g(x)\) and \(h(x)\) with respect to \(x\) and then evaluating the new limit.</p>
<p>In this case, we have \(\displaystyle \lim_{{x \to 0}} f(x)\) in the form \(\frac{0}{0}\), so we can use L'Hôpital's Rule:</p>
<p>\[\lim_{{x \to 0}} f(x) = \lim_{{x \to 0}} \frac{\cos(x) - 1}{x} = \lim_{{x \to 0}} \frac{-\sin(x)}{1} = -\sin(0) = 0\]</p>
<p>Thus, the value of \(\displaystyle \lim_{{x \to 0}} f(x)\) is \(0\).</p>]]></text>
